#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/id #uuid "a788a6e6-8fb4-5657-912d-2eb8295a297f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4800" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\in V$. Given that $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent\n2. $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly independent\n3. $\\{\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n$x_{1}(4v_{1}) + x_{2}(5v_{2}) + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$,\nwhich, in turn, gives\n$(4x_{1})v_{1} + (5x_{2})v_{2} + x_{3}v_{3}|= 0$.\nSince $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n${4x_{1} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$.\nThe unique solution of the system is $x_1=x_2=x_3=0$, which means that $\\{4\\mathbf{v}_1, 5\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly independent.\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n$1 \\cdot \\mathbf{0} + 0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + 0 \\cdot (2\\mathbf{v}_2) = \\mathbf{0}$.\nAs a result, $x_1\\mathbf{0}+x_2\\mathbf{v}_1+x_3(2\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{0}$ has a non-zero solution. This means that $\\{\\mathbf{0}, \\mathbf{v}_1, 2\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $x_1\\mathbf{v}_2+x_2\\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ must have only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0$. If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3=\\mathbf{0}$, then we obtain\n$0 \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_1 + a \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_2 + b \\cdot \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$,\nwhich would mean that $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3\\}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124214 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdde35e9-7101-55f2-8452-ec1ef2d17d54" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Matrix Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7f0b6987-0ca3-5610-ab7b-6630c0f70191" :question/math-academy-id "e-4801" :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_1}{x_2}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1A+x_2B=O$, where $O$ is the $2 \\times 2$ zero-matrix." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2$ not both zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1A+x_2B=O\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = 0\n$$\n\nthe set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} + x_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & 0 \\\\ -2x_{1} & 4x_{1}\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}3x_{2} & 5x_{2} \\\\ 2x_{2} & -6x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + 3x_{2} & 5x_{2} \\\\ -2x_{1} + 2x_{2} & 4x_{1} - 6x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0; - 2x_{1} + 2x_{2} = 0; 4x_{1} - 6x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + 2R_{1} \\\\ R_{4}:=R_{4} + (-4)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -18 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-\\frac{8}{5})R_{2} \\\\\n&= R_{4}:=R_{4} + \\frac{18}{5}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{x_{1} + 3x_{2} = 0; 5x_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution $x_1=x_2=0$.\n\nConsequently, $\\big\\{A, B \\big\\}$ is linearly independent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124215 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "99ce8f12-ee79-5384-8eb9-e9bfbb2534c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficients That Show Linear Dependence Among Polynomial Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "b8c2902e-9062-5417-9d19-a6a9dba986a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-4803" :question/problem "Consider the set $\\big\\{p_1(t), p_2(t),p_3(t) \\big\\}$ of polynomials from $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, where\n$p_1(t)=1+t, \\qquad p_2(t)=-2t-4, \\qquad p_3(t)=-2t-3$.\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}$ from a non-zero solution of $x_1 \\cdot p_1(t)+x_2 \\cdot p_2(t) + x_3 \\cdot p_3(t)=0$." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist $x_1,x_2,x_3$ not all zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1p_1(t)+x_2p_2(t)+x_3p_3(t)=0\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0\n$$\n\nthe set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$, and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1}(1 + t) + x_{2}(-2t - 4) + x_{3}(-2t - 3) &= 0 \\\\\n(x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3}) + (x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 2x_{3})t &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0; x_{1} - 2x_{2} - 2x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, $x_3$ is a free variable. The system is now\n\n$$\n{x_{1} - 4x_{2} - 3x_{3} = 0; 2x_{2} + x_{3} = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2=-\\dfrac 12x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1-4\\left(-\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)-3x_3=0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad x_1=x_3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\nFinally, for any\n\n$$\nx_3 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1}=\\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 12x_3\\right)}{x_3}=-\\dfrac 12\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8785e6cd-943f-57bf-89f8-001fef966d96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121913} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b46e6e-2a53-5ce1-8620-8ac8b7d40179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121914} {:answer/id #uuid "dd52f27d-2301-5623-a66d-7601da1e90b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121915} {:answer/id #uuid "6aaaf83b-3100-5a2b-9d0d-b855a41de89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121916} {:answer/id #uuid "75e0b1df-0149-5a82-a105-8205251c3493" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121917}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd52f27d-2301-5623-a66d-7601da1e90b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121915} :answer-field/id #uuid "73cbae3a-c1c9-536e-b42e-a26c524f060f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121912}] :question/id #uuid "1d95ba8a-32c3-52ab-a4a0-101091efe61a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35706" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors $v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4} ∈ V$. Given that ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}}$ is linearly independent and $v_{4} = 3v_{1} - 2v_{2} + 5v_{3}$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v_{2}$ is a linear combination of $v_{1}$, $v_{3}$, and $v_{4}$\n2. $x_{1}v_{1} + x_{2}v_{2} + x_{3}v_{3} + x_{4}v_{4} = 0$ has only zero solution $x_{1} = x_{2} = x_{3} = x_{4} = 0$\n3. ${v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}, v_{4}}$ is a linearly independent set"}]}
